// `mutable([])` takes its element type from the context, because `[]` is the one literal that
// carries none of its own. `Map<K, V> {}` and `Set<T> {}` write their types, so `mutable` over
// either already worked; the array literal was the gap, and the error used to tell an author who
// had written `MutableArray<int>` one phrase to the left to "add a type annotation".
//
// The hook that does this is the fourth of its kind -- ok/err, channel_new and a bare Some/None
// have the same shape -- and like them it has to be registered at every site that offers a type.
// There are three: a val/var initializer, a call argument, and check_expr_expecting, which covers
// returns, struct literal fields and `with` overrides. Registering it at one looked done and was
// not, which is why all four positions are exercised below.
struct Holder { items: MutableArray<int> }
fn take(b: MutableArray<int>) -> int { return len(b) }
fn make() -> MutableArray<string> { return mutable([]) }
fn main() {
    var v: MutableArray<int> = mutable([])
    push(v, 1)
    print(snapshot(v))

    print(take(mutable([])))
    print(len(make()))

    val h = Holder { items: mutable([]) }
    push(h.items, 7)
    print(snapshot(h.items))
}
